This is the full case study (14 min). The [short version (7 min)](/work/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/) covers the two main problems.

{% partial file="./inso-shared/numbers.mdoc" /%}

## Framing the problem

INSO operates in 20+ countries, many with patchy or no internet coverage, so alerts go out by SMS, and by email for longer formats. The alerts themselves were clear. Around them, a few things were missing. This version follows these four.

{% partial file="./inso-shared/problems-1-2.mdoc" /%}

{% problem n="3" title="SMS and email gave INSO no way to see how alerts were used" %}
Emails weren't always opened, and SMS has no real read receipts, so country offices only learned how their alerts did by asking. {% sourceline text="Raised by the country offices." /%}
{% /problem %}

{% problem n="4" title="Composing and sending meant a laptop" %}
INSO staff wrote and sent alerts through the same system, which they found impractical on the move, in meetings or at events, and sometimes without internet. And every SMS cost the country offices money. {% sourceline text="Raised by the country offices." /%}
{% /problem %}

## Users

**Three groups: two receive alerts, one sends them**

{% usertable photos="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/01-user-ngo-field-worker.jpg|projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/02-user-security-ops-staff.jpg||projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/03-user-inso-staff.jpg" alts="An NGO field worker with a backpack walking across a car park|A security officer on a satellite phone next to a field vehicle||An INSO staff member writing an alert on a laptop" %}
| Who | Needs | Pains before the app |
|---|---|---|
| **NGO field workers** · In the field, on the move, exposed to risk, sometimes with no internet | Stay updated while moving · Focus only on what affects their safety: the road, where they're going, where they sleep | Only whole regions, activated by their NGO's account admin on a laptop · Alerts lost among every other SMS and email |
| **Security staff** · In an office, keeping an eye on teams who travel, advising them by SMS or a call | Monitor specific areas remotely · Track patterns and ongoing situations | Couldn't follow a place they weren't in · No radius or custom area, like the compound where staff live |
| **Shared** · Both groups | Quickly understand what's happening nearby · Trust the accuracy of alerts · Stay updated in real time | High pressure, little time to read · Trust depends on alerts that are quick to read · Phone checks are a security risk |
| **INSO staff in the country offices** · Collect, validate, compose and send the alerts | Compose and send quickly, from anywhere, with or without data · See how partners use and experience the alerts, maps, filters and saved alerts, without having to ask · Reduce the cost of SMS | Composing and sending meant a laptop and the Partners Portal, impractical on the move · No way to tell whether alerts were read or used, only what people said when asked · SMS was costly |
{% /usertable %}

## The project when I joined

{% partial file="./inso-shared/project-when-i-joined.mdoc" /%}

{% ab v="a" %}
What first caught my eye was that it didn't look like INSO, with brown and beige everywhere and no INSO blue. But the more I looked, the more I wanted to put some structure around the problem itself.

The engineers had already explored part of the problem: SMS was costly, more and more NGO workers were using smartphones as work phones, and internet coverage was growing in several of INSO's countries. That combination opened up several opportunities that called for an INSO Alerts mobile app. Not another app for the sake of having an app, people don't need that, but the one way to give people a say over what reaches them, to let INSO staff compose and send from anywhere, and to see how alerts are actually used. It's an alternative to SMS and email, not a replacement.

I couldn't do research the traditional way, so I had to be realistic about where I could help: taking things from the first draft that worked, even without data, and checking the rest through a heuristic evaluation, interviews and Maze tests with country office colleagues, then a survey with partners after launch. {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%} of a full research phase. There was no baseline to compare against either, since SMS and email couldn't be tracked.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
What first stood out was that it didn't look like INSO, with brown and beige everywhere and no INSO blue. But the more I looked, the clearer it became that the problem itself needed structure, so that's where I started.

The engineers had already explored part of the problem: SMS was costly, more and more NGO workers were using smartphones as work phones, and internet coverage was growing in several of INSO's countries. That combination opened up several opportunities that called for an INSO Alerts mobile app. Not another app for the sake of having an app, people don't need that, but the one way to give people a say over what reaches them, to let INSO staff compose and send from anywhere, and to see how alerts are actually used. An alternative to SMS and email, not a replacement.

Traditional research wasn't an option, so I had to be realistic about where I could help: keeping what worked in the first draft, even without data, and checking the rest through a heuristic evaluation, interviews and Maze tests with country office colleagues, then a survey with partners after launch. {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%} of a full research phase. There was no baseline to compare against either, since SMS and email couldn't be tracked.
{% /ab %}

**Tracking and testing from the start.** I advised the team to discuss usage with INSO colleagues in the country offices and define tracking metrics that would help understand the usage of the app and help adapt and improve it continuously. I also proposed testing each feature, discussing the findings and agreeing on a course of action before building and shipping.

**Why receiving came first.** Receiving, for NGO field workers and security staff, was prioritised as the main concern. INSO wanted to pilot the app with partners first, and to invest in composing and sending only once management was satisfied with how it did. That green light came later.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-3.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-4.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-5.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-6.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-7.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-8.jpg" alts="The alerts list.|Filters: programmes and alerts with a location only.|The date picker with quick options.|The list, filtered.|The map in Explore mode.|An alert opened from the map.|Proximity: a radius around a place.|Geofence: an area drawn on the map." caption="High-level overview of the app structure and key interactions around alerts." /%}

## Login and onboarding

**Users were asked to act before understanding what the app does**

{% ab v="a" %}
A quick heuristic evaluation of the startup and login screen turned up a few things to fix. The app opened straight on a login form and asked for location access before anyone had logged in, with no hint of why. It never said what the app was for. The login itself was hard to use too, with a barely visible button, an "Invalid email" error before anyone had typed, how to get an account explained in a tiny note, and no way to reset a password. Country office colleagues believed a welcome email would be enough to explain things, but someone could install the app today and only open it a month later when an alert comes in, and by then that email is long gone. So the explanation had to live inside the app.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
I ran a quick heuristic evaluation of the startup and login screen, and it turned up a few things to fix. The app opened straight on a login form and asked for location access before anyone had logged in, with no hint of why. It never said what the app was for. The login itself was hard to use too, with a barely visible button, an "Invalid email" error before anyone had typed, how to get an account explained in a tiny note, and no way to reset a password. Country office colleagues believed a welcome email would be enough to explain things. I disagreed: someone could install the app today and only open it a month later when an alert comes in, and by then that email is long gone. So the explanation had to live inside the app.
{% /ab %}

{% reactions set="inso-login" /%}

{% block icon="did" title="Explain first, ask for location later" %}
Here is how I approached each of the main problems from my heuristic evaluation of the startup and login screen.
{% /block %}

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| Location access was asked first, before anyone had logged in and with no explanation | Location access is now asked only after, once people know what it's for |
| The app never said what it was for | Two short onboarding screens now come before the login |
| The login button was barely visible, and "Invalid email" showed before anyone had typed | The login got the brand back. It starts with just an email and a clear Continue button, and the validation stopped shouting "Invalid email" before anyone had typed a thing |
| How to get an account was a tiny note, and there was no way to reset a password | "Don't have an account? Learn more" now sits under the form in readable text and leads to the registration page on ngosafety.org, with a "Forgot password?" link and a show/hide toggle on the password |

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-02.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-03.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-04.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-05.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-06.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-login-01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-login-02.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The INSO splash screen.|Onboarding, 1 of 2: stay informed and safe while on the move.|Onboarding, 1 of 2, ready to move on.|Onboarding, 2 of 2: monitor any location remotely.|Onboarding, 2 of 2, in the dark map style.|The new login: Let's get started, with an email field and a Continue button.|The login's second step: email and password, a show/hide toggle, Log in and Forgot password." captions="The app opens on the INSO logo, and two short onboarding screens come before the login.|Two short onboarding screens now come before the login: what the app is for.|Two short onboarding screens now come before the login: what the app is for.|The second onboarding screen: monitoring a place you're not in.|The second onboarding screen: monitoring a place you're not in.|The login got the brand back: just an email and a clear Continue button, and \"Don't have an account? Learn more\" in readable text under the form.|Then the password, with a show/hide toggle and a \"Forgot password?\" link." /%}

{% reactions set="inso-login-result" /%}

{% block source="24 of 31 rated installation and onboarding 4 or 5 out of 5 (average 4.1/5) · post-launch partner survey · no earlier version to compare with" %}
Most partners found getting started easy.
{% /block %}

