## Alerts

**The main screen had all the information, but it was hard to read and scan**

{% block title="Eight alerts shared one screen" %}
Eight alerts shared one screen, with titles of 12 to 16 words in a tiny column and small dates and distances competing for attention. The engineers' idea was to show as much as possible without scrolling, so the question became: see everything at once, or read each alert easily? My first thought was "where should I be looking?" There was no way to search by place either, and no profile or way to reach terms, FAQs or info about the app, just a settings icon at the top right.
{% /block %}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-old-alerts-screen.jpg" frame="before" size="text" alt="The old alerts list, annotated: inconsistent typography, low spacing between items, and location, distance and time not prioritised." caption="Alerts screen ==before== redesign, showing density and unclear hierarchy" /%}

{% tablebox label="table" %}
| Alerts list, former design | Redesign approach |
|---|---|
| Five type styles competed in a 70px row: bold all caps titles, small caps locations, all caps dates, tiny right-aligned distance and time | One title style in sentence case, and a clear secondary level for the rest |
| Location, the thing people scan for, was the smallest element, under a four-line title | Location first, then a short title, then time |
| Alerts were separated by a hairline with almost no padding, so rows merged | More space and a clearer boundary between rows |
| No search, no filter and no account area, just an unlabelled orange circle and a settings icon | Search at the top, a simpler filter, and an entry for the profile, terms and help |
| Every alert had the same warning icon, with no cue for new or unseen alerts and no way to save | A marker for unseen alerts, a Recent Alerts block, and a save button on each alert |
{% /tablebox %}

Saved alerts weren't in the first draft. I asked the country office colleagues, who stood in for partners, how people could keep track of the alerts they care about when they all mix together, and we landed on pinning them. It started as one Saved page in the bottom menu, next to Alerts and Settings.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/01-alerts_first_view.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/04-alerts_search_results.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/06-alerts_filter_off.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/07-alerts_filter_date_picker.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/08-alerts_filtered.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/11-map_explore_loaded.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The new alerts list: location first, then a short title, then time.|Search results for Ukraine.|The filter: programmes and regions, and a date range.|The date picker, with quick options like last week.|The list, filtered.|The map with the alerts in the area." caption="The new alerts list: built for scanning, with search at the top, a simpler filter and a list/map toggle." /%}

## Filtering - Addressing problem 1

