{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-flow-2-ut-test-01.jpg" related=true alt="Maze results for filtering alerts by location: the task scenario next to the screens testers went through, with a 100% success rate." caption="Task-based usability test scenario and results for filtering alerts by location" /%}

## Alert detail

**I am in an alert. What information will help me the most?**

{% reactions set="inso-alert-detail" /%}

{% tablebox label="table" %}
| Alert detail, former design | Redesign approach |
|---|---|
| The map filled the top third, so the facts came after it | A summary first (what, where, when, how far), with the map moved lower |
| Two pins, teal and red, with no legend | One pin, with the place name and distance below |
| The latest update and advice were buried under five lower-value fields | Full location, incident type, latest update, latest advice, in the order the interviews showed |
| The date and time showed three times in three formats, and one field was empty | One date and time, and the empty field is gone |
| A raw system label (ALERT_UPDATE) and an email address served as the banner | A clear "Alert update" tag, and an "Alerts history" section |
| Body text was justified with uneven margins, and headings looked like body text | Left-aligned text with consistent margins, and clear headings with space around them |
{% /tablebox %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/01-single-alert.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/02-single-alert.jpg" fulls="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/01-single-alert-full.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/02-single-alert-full.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The redesigned alert detail: an Alert tag and the place, the title, date, time and distance on top, then full location, incident type, latest update and latest advice under clear headings, with the map and its one pin lower down.|An alert update: an Alert update tag on top, the same order of facts, and an Alerts history section at the end listing the earlier alerts." captions="A summary first (what, where, when, how far), one date and time, then the latest update and advice in the order the interviews showed, with the map lower and one pin. Enlarge to see the whole screen.|An update gets a clear \"Alert update\" tag instead of a system label, and the earlier alerts sit in an \"Alerts history\" section. Left-aligned text with clear headings throughout." /%}

### Usability vs security: keeping alerts safe made the app harder to use

Alerts are sensitive, so what people can copy or see was a long conversation with country office colleagues, and so was keeping the app discreet if someone looks at the phone. Finding the middle ground was tricky. It isn't fully settled, because the solutions to security concerns kept creating usability problems somewhere else: no copying or screenshots, several layers to get into the app, no Face ID. Partners named the copy and screenshot block as their biggest frustration.

## Maps - Addressing problem 2

{% partial file="./inso-shared/maps-explore.mdoc" /%}

Testing also caught two smaller things: scrolling an alert also moved the map, and alerts without a location never showed up on it, so list and map counts didn't match.

### Proximity: how do I stay informed on anything happening around me while on the move and how do I monitor an area remotely?

Proximity shows alerts within a radius of your live location, or of a pin you drop somewhere else. In the engineers' version, it sat in a separate settings screen, with nothing to explain it. People expect to narrow down a list where the list is, with a filter or a button like Airbnb's Map button, not in settings.

So in the **first iteration** of the redesign, Proximity moved into the map as one of its modes, with one short tooltip per mode saying what it does.

{% ab v="a" %}
The first version had two ways to set that radius: on the map, and with a slider in the filter menu a bit like dating apps do. Most testers went to the map through the Map button on the alerts list, so the slider went and the radius now lives in one place.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
The first version had two ways to set that radius: on the map, and with a slider in the filter menu a bit like dating apps do. Most testers went to the map through the Map button on the alerts list, so I removed the slider and kept the radius in one place.
{% /ab %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/01-map_explore.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/02-map_prox_view_01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/03-map_prox_view_intruct_1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/04-map_prox_view_default.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/05-map_prox_view_active.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/06-alerts_options_applied_indication.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The map in Explore mode.|Proximity, with Your location and Custom location, and a radius slider.|The single tooltip: Alerts by proximity.|Proximity: a radius around your location.|Proximity: the radius set, with the alerts inside it.|Back on the list: a 50km proximity filter is on." caption="The first iteration: Proximity in the map, with one tooltip per mode saying what it does." /%}

{% reactions set="inso-proximity-unsure" /%}

{% block source="From usability testing sessions and surveys with country offices, using a design prototype" %}
6 participants set a radius around a place they weren't in in 53 seconds on average, with an average ease score of 6.8 out of 10. It was slow, and some couldn't tell live location from custom location.
{% /block %}

#### Illusion of Explanatory Depth (IOED)

The above fixed where to find Proximity and understand what the feature is meant to be used for **but not how to use it**. Thinking aloud, most testers demonstrated a fair understanding of the feature's intro text, then struggled with a task that used its two settings, live and custom location.

The tooltip said what Proximity does, which seemed clear to my biased thinking. The concept of **Illusion of Explanatory Depth** says it's one thing to understand something and another to explain the step by step and be able to put it to practice when applicable.

In the second iteration, the single tooltip became a short three-step tutorial: nothing too long, to avoid killing engagement, but enough to show how to use the mode.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/01-map_explore.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/02-map_prox_view_01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/03-map_prox_view_intruct_1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/04-map_prox_view_intruct_2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/05-map_prox_view_intruct_3.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/06-map_prox_view_default.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/07-map_prox_view_active.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/08-alerts_options_applied_indication.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The map in Explore mode.|Proximity, with Your location and Custom location.|Tutorial, 1 of 3: see what's around you.|Tutorial, 2 of 3.|Tutorial, 3 of 3.|Proximity: a radius around your location.|Proximity: the radius set, with the alerts inside it.|Back on the list: a 50km proximity filter is on." caption="The second iteration: a three-step tutorial shows how to use live and custom location." /%}

{% reactions set="inso-proximity-maybe" /%}

{% block source="Interviews with 5 country office colleagues who hadn't seen the app before · updated design and prototype" %}
The tutorial cleared up the difference between live and custom location, and colleagues were more satisfied with the feature.
{% /block %}

### Geofence: how do I follow one specific area, like the compound where staff live?

Draw a shape on the map to cover the exact area, and keep getting alerts from inside it.

It was called Polygon, and saving meant pressing Back. It now has a Confirm button, a three-step tutorial, and a name that says what it does.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/former-geofence.jpg" frame="before" size="text" badges="37,86.3|50,30.5|57.2,43.7|61.5,79|60.9,14.5" spots="38.3,61.8,83.6,89.4|30.4,69.8,21,40|45,65.4,40.5,53.2|63.2,69.2,77.6,89.2|40,60,15.4,19.4" alt="The former Polygon screen: a map with a drawn shape, two unlabelled orange buttons, a grey note saying press the back button to save the polygon filter, and a Proximity / Polygon switch at the top." caption="The ==former== Polygon mode, numbered to match the table below." /%}

**Polygon mode (Geofence)**

| Geofence (Polygon) former design | Redesign approach (see slideshow below) |
|---|---|
| {% n v="1" /%}Saving meant pressing the Back button ("Press the back button to save the polygon filter"). It was hard to find, and Back normally cancels. | An explicit Confirm button, and a "Geofence established" message afterwards |
| {% n v="2" /%}No instructions. The first screen was just a map, with no hint to tap to drop pins, or that zooming first matters. | A three-step tutorial: zoom, drop pins, confirm |
| {% n v="3" /%}No result feedback. You drew a shape but couldn't tell how many alerts it contained. | "76 alerts in the selected area", with the alert markers inside the shape |
| {% n v="4" /%}Two unlabelled orange buttons (an undo arrow and a "+"). Their purpose wasn't clear, and orange didn't match the rest of the app. | An undo button, plus labelled "Clear all" and "Edit geofence" buttons |
| {% n v="5" /%}The name "Polygon" is a geometry term, not a task. | Renamed Geofence, one of three clear tabs: Explore, Proximity, Geofence |

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/01-map_view_geofence_1_blank.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/02-map_view_geofence_instruct.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/03-map_view_geofence_instruct_first_line.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/04-map_view_geofence_instruct_geofence.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/06-map_view_geofence_1_step_2-2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/07-map_view_geofence_1_step_2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/08-map_view_geofence_1_step_2-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/flow/09-geofence_based_alerts.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The Geofence tab of the map, before drawing.|Tutorial, step 1: select your zoom level.|Tutorial, step 2: press to drop your pins.|Tutorial, step 3: confirm your selection.|A pin dropped, with the Confirm button and undo.|Pins joined into an area, with the Confirm button and the number of alerts in it.|The area confirmed, with its alerts, Clear all and Edit geofence.|The alerts list with a Geofence established message." captions="The Geofence tab, one of three map modes.|Tutorial, step 1: select your zoom level.|Tutorial, step 2: press to drop your pins.|Tutorial, step 3: confirm your selection.|Dropping a pin, with Confirm and undo within reach.|Pins joined into an area, ready to confirm.|Confirmed: the alerts inside the area, with Clear all and Edit geofence.|Back in the list, “Geofence established” confirms it." /%}

{% ab v="a" %}
It was also the hardest to learn: 5 of 8 testers finished the task but none of them took the intended path. Two asked for a video, which is heavy on weak connections, so Geofence got the same kind of three-step animated tutorial instead.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
It was also the hardest to learn: 5 of 8 testers finished the task but none of them took the intended path. Two asked for a video, which is heavy on weak connections, so I gave Geofence the same kind of three-step animated tutorial instead.
{% /ab %}

{% block source="From the same 5 interviews with country office colleagues" %}
Colleagues understood Geofence, but saw it as relevant only to those who work actively with maps.
{% /block %}

### What people actually use

{% block source="Usage tracking since launch · partners ranked them in the same order in the post-launch survey, with Geofence last (9 of 30)" %}
About 1 in 6 people who used the app have opened a map, and Explore takes most of that traffic, with Proximity and Geofence well behind.
{% /block %}

## After the first release

**Continuous feedback kept shaping the app after launch**

{% ab v="a" %}
The project didn't stop here, since continuous feedback from country office colleagues kept shaping the app after launch:
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
The project didn't stop here. I kept shaping the app after launch with continuous feedback from country office colleagues:
{% /ab %}

- Unseen alerts pointed out, a new Recent Alerts block, and a restyled date picker with quick options (last 3 days, last week, last month) that shows clearly when a filter is on.
- Animated tutorials in every map mode.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-2.png, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-3.jpg" alts="The alerts list while browsing.|A New alerts button appears at the top of the list.|The list with a Recent Alerts block on top, holding the alerts not seen yet." caption="New alerts pointed out live while you browse the list, with the Recent Alerts block separating what's new from what you've read." /%}

### What about creating and sending alerts from the app?

This was part of the problem from the start, but we agreed to focus on the NGO side first. With management sign-off I designed the first version in early 2025, and it was released in September 2025 together with login through an INSO account: a simple form with a map pin and distribution lists, plus updates to existing alerts.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-compose.jpg" alt="Composing an alert: the alerts list with an INSO ALERT label and a megaphone button, the empty form, the filled form with location and distribution lists, and the sent confirmation." caption="Composing an INSO ALERT from the phone, from the megaphone on the alerts list to the confirmation. Alert text, place names, the map and list sizes are blurred." /%}

