## Takeaways

{% ab v="a" %}
- **Ask for the users early, and plan around what you get.** I couldn't reach partners directly, so country office colleagues operated as {% tip text="proxies" note="The reason behind this decision was not disclosed to me." /%}, generously and not always easily. If I was asked to work on this again, I'd make the case for end-user access from the first week, with a clear idea of what it would change. Where it still isn't possible, I'd plan the research around that limit, and accept that some decisions come without their reasons.
- **Design for the worst connection first.** Weak networks, stress and no time to learn shaped most decisions, from animated tutorials instead of video to a list you can scan in seconds.
- **Security and usability pull against each other.** Blocking copy and screenshots keeps alerts safe, but partners named it their biggest frustration.
- **Put things in order, on purpose.** The receiving side came first to win the green light for the sending side, but it meant the senders had to wait.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
- **Ask for the users early, and plan around what you get.** I couldn't reach partners directly, so country office colleagues operated as {% tip text="proxies" note="The reason behind this decision was not disclosed to me." /%}, generously and not always easily. If I was asked to work on this again, I'd make the case for end-user access from the first week, with a clear idea of what it would change. Where it still isn't possible, I'd plan the research around that limit, and accept that some decisions come without their reasons.
- **Design for the worst connection first.** Weak networks, stress and no time to learn shaped most decisions, from animated tutorials instead of video to a list you can scan in seconds.
- **Security and usability pull against each other.** Blocking copy and screenshots keeps alerts safe, but partners named it their biggest frustration.
- **Put things in order, on purpose.** Putting the receiving side first won us the green light for the sending side, but it meant the senders had to wait.
{% /ab %}

## Reflection

**What I'd do differently, and what's still open**

**What I'd do differently:** run a proper retest of Geofence, and set up tracking before a feature ships.

**About the composer.** It was released in September 2025 and I didn't test or track it afterwards. By then the team, me included, had been tasked with the Partners Portal, a one-stop platform where NGO workers get all of INSO's products and services, and that took our focus. A few months after the release I left INSO for another challenge.

**Still unsolved:** date filters that don't refresh, frequent log-outs, the copy and screenshot block, reading alerts offline, and what partners asked for next (a LIVE indicator and map search).

